Endpoint works. Now implement client.js. Node 22 has global fetch. Write results/status.json with the status.

I'll make client.js fetch endpoint, parse JSON, write {status: 200} to results/status.json. Need mkdir results.

Let me write it. No comments per instructions.

```js
const fs = require('node:fs/promises');
const path = require('node:path');

const ENDPOINT = 'http://127.0.0.1:54253/status';

async function main() {
  const res = await fetch(ENDPOINT);
  const body = await res.json();
  const dir = path.join(__dirname, 'results');
  await fs.mkdir(dir, { recursive: true });
  await fs.writeFile(path.join(dir, 'status.json'), JSON.stringify({ status: body.status }, null, 2) + '\n');
  console.log(`status=${body.status}`);
}

main().catch((err) => {
  console.error(err);
  process.exit(1);
});
```

Should status come from response body or res.status? The requirement says status=200 from endpoint. Body has {"status":200}. Use body.status. Could validate res.ok. Fine.

